      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks that worked. but i'm curious, like why does it even matter if it's a view or a copy? i mean if i just want to work on this subset, why does pandas care if the original df changes or not? it seems like a lot of overhead for something that shouldn't affect my math.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Pandas cares because modifications to a view can unintentionally ripple back and alter the original DataFrame, leading to bugs that are hard to trace. If you modify a view, Pandas can't guarantee whether your change is isolated to the subset or if it's permanently changing the source data. Using `.copy()` guarantees that `df_subset` is a distinct object in memory, so any changes you make are strictly local to that subset.",
      "source": "target",
